#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079527} :activity/id #uuid "b8eeb531-1cc3-5a66-b9a3-821d7aefa496" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049823 :topic/id #uuid "168508a9-54ad-56a7-94f7-54369acb9706" :topic/math-academy-id 652} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079527 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079710 :tutorial/content "We know how to evaluate expressions like $2^3, 2^2, 2^1$, and even $2^0.$ But what if the power is negative? How would we evaluate $2^{-1},$ for example?\n\nTo help us with that, let's go back to the results table for powers of $2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{4} & =16 \\\\ {2}^{3} & =8 \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {2}^{1} & =2 \\\\ {2}^{0} & =1 \\\\ {2}^{-1} & =◻\\,?\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is divided by $2$ every time. If we follow the same pattern, we find that \n\n$$\n2^{\\color{red}{-1}} = {\\color{blue}{1}}\\div 2 = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ is just the reciprocal of $2.$ So, raising a number to the power of $-1$ is the *same* as finding its reciprocal!" :tutorial/id #uuid "1a1e2f61-f43c-5311-914b-4898ea6cd4eb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8016 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f1b1c942-6488-5737-81b4-60277d491717" :step/math-academy-id 51347 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079528}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079528 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078574 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078150 :question/id #uuid "6b6bbf54-883b-5890-b016-95a5a02f56c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-12785"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d14f7dad-9602-57e7-83ee-cb518f9b573d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050749}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479892}] :knowledge-point/title "Raising an Integer to the Power of Negative One"} :step/id #uuid "9cfe4369-690